WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.480
You just ate a sandwich, and your leg muscles are well stocked with sugar.

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.720
So why can't a muscle simply burn that sugar to pull?

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.400
The answer is a small molecule called ATP.

4
00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:18.543
Every working cell spends energy.

5
00:00:19.883 --> 00:00:20.903
A muscle cell pulls.

6
00:00:22.263 --> 00:00:25.503
A nerve cell pumps charged particles across its membrane.

7
00:00:26.923 --> 00:00:28.683
Almost every cell builds proteins.

8
00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:32.783
None of these jobs runs on glucose directly.

9
00:00:34.063 --> 00:00:38.843
They run on ATP, a molecule that carries energy to the jobs that use it.

10
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:42.443
ATP has three phosphate groups in a row.

11
00:00:43.663 --> 00:00:46.523
When it reacts with water, the end phosphate comes off.

12
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:49.943
What is left is ADP, with only two.

13
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:54.903
That reaction releases the energy the muscle uses to pull.

14
00:00:55.967 --> 00:01:00.787
Here's the catch. A cell keeps only a small supply of ATP.

15
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:05.687
Watch that supply while the muscle keeps pulling, again and again.

16
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.560
Suppose nothing rebuilt ATP from ADP.

17
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.200
What would happen to this supply, and to the pulling?

18
00:01:19.817 --> 00:01:22.997
The supply would soon run low, and the pulling would stop.

19
00:01:24.157 --> 00:01:27.897
So a working cell rebuilds ATP from ADP all the time.

20
00:01:29.237 --> 00:01:31.117
Putting the phosphate back takes energy.

21
00:01:32.397 --> 00:01:34.937
That energy comes from taking glucose apart.

22
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.520
Glucose holds energy in the way its atoms are bonded.

23
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.060
Taking it apart starts in the cytoplasm, the jelly-like inside of the cell.

24
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.200
These first steps split glucose in two and rebuild a little ATP.

25
00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:55.063
With oxygen, the two halves move into a mitochondrion.

26
00:01:56.263 --> 00:02:00.403
This organelle finishes the job, down to carbon dioxide and water.

27
00:02:01.743 --> 00:02:03.963
Most of the ATP is rebuilt here.

28
00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:06.957
Count what one glucose gave back.

29
00:02:08.057 --> 00:02:10.777
With oxygen, it rebuilds about thirty ATP.

30
00:02:12.177 --> 00:02:14.037
Some energy also leaves as heat.

31
00:02:15.257 --> 00:02:17.657
That is one reason you warm up when you exercise.

32
00:02:18.817 --> 00:02:20.217
Now the loop closes.

33
00:02:21.677 --> 00:02:23.317
Work turns ATP into ADP.

34
00:02:24.857 --> 00:02:27.457
Respiration turns ADP back into ATP.

35
00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:31.617
Round and round, for as long as the cell is alive.

36
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.720
This whole process is cellular respiration.

37
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.580
Glucose and oxygen go in.

38
00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:45.650
Carbon dioxide and water come out, with energy, much of it captured in ATP.

39
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.940
ATP is not energy itself.

40
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.060
It is a molecule that carries it.

41
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.960
But a sprinting leg can need ATP faster than oxygen-using respiration can rebuild

42
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:03.080
it. Yeast sealed in bread dough gets almost no oxygen at all.

43
00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:05.340
What happens then?

44
00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.720
Switch the oxygen off in the simulation and find out.
